=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The campus DNS server (`named`/BIND) running on `srv1` (10.5.0.4) is misconfigured
with `recursion no` in `/etc/bind/named.conf`. This means the server will only
answer authoritative queries for its own `jeremysitlab.com` zone; every other
domain lookup — google.com, youtube.com, and any other external name — is
immediately rejected with a REFUSED response.

Every client device in the network (pc1, pc2, pc3, laptop1, srv1's own VLAN peers)
has a single nameserver entry pointing to 10.5.0.4. The moment a user's browser or
application performs a DNS resolution for any external hostname, the DNS server
refuses the query. With no IP address returned, the TCP connection never opens,
and the browser spins until it times out. This is indistinguishable to the end
user from a dead internet link — hence the misdirected suspicion toward the WAN
"internet box."

The underlying network path is in perfect health: OSPF has fully converged, the
default route is properly propagated from r1 throughout the campus (csw1, csw2,
and all distribution switches hold valid default routes), NAT masquerade rules
on r1 are functional (packet counters confirm traffic is being translated), and
raw IP connectivity to the simulated ISP loopback addresses is confirmed from
multiple clients with 0% loss.

Root cause: `recursion no` in `/etc/bind/named.conf` on `srv1`, preventing
resolution of any non-local domain name.

Corrective fix: Edit `/etc/bind/named.conf` on `srv1`, change `recursion no;`
to `recursion yes;`, optionally add `forwarders { <upstream-DNS-IP>; };` pointing
to a DNS resolver reachable via the ISP, then run `rndc reload` (or restart
`named`) to apply the change.
=== END ===
